    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe audit line now works, but the way `membershipNumber` is derived sits in the wrong unit.\n\n**Finding: move the `membershipNumber` derivation onto `Owner` as a derived getter.**\n\nThe `Owner` entity has a well-established, consistent pattern for read-only values derived from its stored fields, each a documented getter:\n- `getTelephoneDisplay()` \u2014 formats `telephone`\n- `getAgeBand()` \u2014 derives from `birthDate`/`registrationDate`\n- `getMembershipPoints()` / `getMembershipLevel()` \u2014 derived tier values\n- `getIdentityKey()` \u2014 joins stored fields into a formatted key\n- `getCheckDigit()` \u2014 derives from `customerCode` (null when unset)\n\nThe new `membershipNumber` is exactly this kind of value: a formatted string built from `customerCode` and `registrationDate`, null-guarded when either is missing. Placing that computation inline inside the AUDIT block of `saveOwner` breaks the pattern and puts domain-formatting logic in a service's logging statement, where a maintainer wouldn't think to look for or reuse it. Note the audit line already logs `owner.getMembershipLevel()` \u2014 a sibling derived getter \u2014 right next to the hand-rolled `membershipNumber`, which makes the inconsistency stark.\n\nSuggested shape:\n\n- Add to `Owner`:\n  ```java\n  /**\n   * The owner's membership number formatted {@code '<customerCode>-M<YY>'} where YY is the\n   * last two digits of the registration year, or null when either the customerCode or the\n   * registrationDate is not set.\n   */\n  public String getMembershipNumber() {\n      if (this.customerCode == null || this.registrationDate == null) {\n          return null;\n      }\n      String yy = String.format(\"%02d\", this.registrationDate.getYear() % 100);\n      return this.customerCode + \"-M\" + yy;\n  }\n  ```\n- Collapse the audit block back to a single call:\n  ```java\n  if (creating) {\n      AUDIT.info(\n          \"owner created: id={} customerCode={} registrationDate={} membershipLevel={} membershipNumber={}\",\n          owner.getId(), owner.getCustomerCode(), owner.getRegistrationDate(),\n          owner.getMembershipLevel(), owner.getMembershipNumber());\n  }\n  ```\n\nThis keeps the derivation next to its siblings, makes it reusable/testable, and restores the audit statement to a plain logging call consistent with how the level is already logged.",
